If you're simulating the passing of time in a browser for visual or functional effects, you can use JavaScript to control the flow of time within your application. Understand that you cannot genuinely "warp time"; you can only simulate the effects or perception of time passing.

Here's an example of how you might simulate 24 hours passing in 10 minutes:

```javascript
// Calculate the speed ratio
// (24 hours * 60 minutes/hour) / 10 minutes = 144x speed
const speed = (24 * 60) / 10;

// Start time (could be now, or a specified date-time)
let simulatedCurrentTime = new Date();

// Function to update the simulated time
function updateSimulatedTime() {
    // Calculate the real elapsed time since last update
    let realTimeNow = new Date();
    let realElapsedTime = realTimeNow - simulatedCurrentTime;

    // Update the simulated time by the real elapsed time multiplied by the speed
    simulatedCurrentTime = new Date(simulatedCurrentTime.getTime() + realElapsedTime * speed);

    // Display the time or do something else
    console.log("Simulated time:", simulatedCurrentTime);

    // Schedule the next update
    setTimeout(updateSimulatedTime, 1000); // updates every real second
}

// Start the simulation
